00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,600
Chapter 16. Nasal consonants. Block the air with your tongue and release it

2
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:11,960
through your nose. We now turn to the three consonants whose sounds come out

3
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:16,940
through the nose. M, N, and the NG combination. They each have one thing in

4
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:21,420
common. Their sound is blocked in the mouth in one of three locations. Two of

5
00:00:21,420 --> 00:00:27,640
them, N and NG, you can't even see, as with R, so they're hard to pick up on. M is the

6
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:33,040
easiest and most obvious, like b. The lips come together and the air can't get out so

7
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:38,980
it has to come through the nose. N is in a position similar to t, but it can't be at

8
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:42,980
all tense. It has to be completely relaxed, filling the whole mouth, touching

9
00:00:42,980 --> 00:00:47,300
the insides of all the teeth, leaving no room for the air to escape except by the

10
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:54,340
nose. NG is back in the throat with G. The back of the tongue presses back and again

11
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:55,760
the air comes out through the nose.
